<Base lang="en" title="Musings"><article class="prose"><p class="label">Writing</p><h1>Musings.</h1><p>Loose, occasional writing that doesn't belong to any collection.</p><div class="ep-layout"><div class="ep-tabs"><button class="ep-tab active" data-ep="0">01 · Graduation Reflections - Preface</button><button class="ep-tab" data-ep="1">02 · A Space Left for You</button></div><div class="ep-panels"><div class="ep-panel active" data-ep="0"><p>The day has finally come. You sit in front of the computer and open that painfully familiar document, the one you've revised more times than you can count.</p><p>You've lost count of how many times you've collapsed in front of the screen crying, but when you open your blurred eyes, what meets them is still the same familiar, blinking cursor.</p><p>At your most useless, you find yourself needing ChatGPT to comfort you.</p><p>Where should the story start? Maybe three years ago. Looking back now, you stand at this end and look at the road you've walked, a thousand feelings rushing up at once, and in the end all you get is a bitter laugh.</p><p>You knew this day would come eventually, but you're still not sure you're ready for it. Having said goodbye to student life, you know clearly that you are no longer the person who once crouched in Hong Kong airport, secretly crying while eating an egg sandwich three years ago. Now you handle things that look thorny with ease, yet you fall apart over the smallest things. "Parting" — whether it's the parting of the living or the parting of death. So many familiar things feel like they could vanish the very next second.</p><p class="scene-break">· · ·</p><p>Take the time you finally managed to get back to Toronto — even though it had only been two weeks, you still noticed the small changes right away. You bought a SOE at the coffee shop by the station, and the coffee, freshly bought, was knocked out of your hand by someone passing by. You listened to their string of "sorry, sorry," and, as you so often do, smiled and said "it's fine." What you didn't let on was that the coffee spilled on the ground shattered your heart into a thousand pieces in that instant. You've always cursed this city as a wreck, but you never quite understood what Toronto meant to you. All you know is that its nickname, "the city," along with another place called "the mountain city" (Montreal), kept appearing again and again in your writing. Maybe you had already become the adult you once dreamed of as a child, but you can no longer get back to that childhood purity. Over these years you figured out the price of growing too fast, so should you keep growing at all? That question has troubled you for a long time. You still haven't reached an answer. You stand in the flood of time, watching the people around you — some hurrying, some sprinting — as if you alone were held in place by an invisible shackle. You step out of the TTC subway station breathing clouds into the cold air, and find that under the familiar amber light, a thin scattering of snow has already begun to fall. You saw Westlife live in Toronto once — a kind of repayment to your childhood self. Your usual record store is on Spadina, your favourite coffee shop is on College, and the bakery you like is on Wellesley. You've always stubbornly loved going to Centre Island, where U of T students often picnic, and so many of the scenes in your own writing are drawn from there. You've seen the fireworks of summer and felt the snow-white of winter. You miss the $3.99 pineapples at the Farmboy near Harbourfront, and the place on Dundas where you used to eat with friends. What did those two-plus years in Toronto actually give you? Even you can't answer that.</p><p class="scene-break">· · ·</p><p>On Christmas Eve you sat in a McDonald's on West Nanjing Road, sipping Coke and nibbling a grilled chicken burger, staring blankly at the crowds passing below, a new cherry McFlurry and half-eaten fries on the table beside you. You realize that eight times out of ten, whenever you come to this city it's for an exam — a chance to actually take the city in slowly like this might be the first time. Still, you remember the stray cats outside SISU's Yifu building, remember the first time you put in contact lenses with shaking hands, remember the thrill of first stepping into that unknown territory. The foreign-language bookstore on Fuzhou Road is the same as ever — four years ago its top-floor ceiling leaked, and four years later it still leaks. You stood up to put your tray on the return rack, and noticed, to your surprise, that the two-seat table you'd been sitting at had already been taken by a couple. It was only then you realized the restaurant's BGM was Eason Chan's "Christmas Knot" — how fitting, you thought with a bitter laugh. You turned, pushed open the door, and stepped back out into Shanghai's winter wind. You looked closely at this city, just as gilded and indulgent as Toronto, closed your eyes, and tried to search for your own possibilities.</p><p>But in the end, what does any of that glitter and indulgence have to do with you?</p><p>You boarded a flight to New York on a whim, and only you knew that the real reason was to satisfy some unrealistic fantasy in your heart. Manhattan's spring wind couldn't blow away the mess of your thoughts. Maybe long before that you'd already decided New York would be your first stop into this country. Pain, unease, confusion, anxiety, at a loss for what to do — you stood in front of the bronze bull on Wall Street, feeling as if every rule that ran the world either had everything to do with you, or nothing at all. In that moment you found yourself envying the people who had genuinely found their own way of living. Centennial Park and all that glamour from a Taylor song tied you to this world through countless invisible threads.</p><p>"So are you sad again?"</p><p>"Yeah. I'm sad again."</p><p>Even after all these years, you still haven't grown at all. Sometimes you really do just want to hide, or just die and be done with it. That's what you think.</p><p>Whether it's the blue-eyed, blond-haired boy you'd only just met, or the blue-eyed, blond-haired girl you'd known for years, they are equally dazzling in your eyes.</p><p>"We have always longed for a miracle to fall on the people we love, but we never once believed we ourselves could deserve happiness." That's a line from something you once wrote, and even now, after all this time, you still believe it. Because you aren't strong enough, self-loathing has simply become a part of who you are.</p><p>You can feel genuinely happy — even cry — over someone else's joy, but you have never once believed you could receive that joy yourself.</p><p>You open your eyes again and find yourself already swaying inside a purple subway line carriage. The announcement is the same mechanical female voice as always; you realize you're about to transfer, so you get off. Stepping onto the green line, you lean against the crowd, close your eyes for a moment's rest, and watch the students passing by, knowing you can never go back to that era. You don't know why your connection to Heluokou runs so deep, or why Wuxi's rush hour is always this packed. When you open your eyes again, you find you've somehow been teleported to the bright yellow line. You've never quite understood why the two and four lines are the same colour in all three of the cities that matter to you. But the line that connects you and that person is still the same glaring red and radiant yellow of the number one line. This illusion leaves you constantly hazy, unsure which city you're actually standing in. Honestly, you've never known whether this feeling is good or bad. You've always been torn — drawn to Western freedom and equality, yet unable to fully shake off the East Asian yoke placed on you. You suddenly remember a professor mentioning the "rat race" in some senior-year class, and think that maybe you'd already become part of it without even realizing. You want to push forward with everything you have, but the moment you do, the sadness of the past comes flooding back like a tide. You, who never tire of the smell of fresh book ink, have never been stingy with your love and impulse for certain things — but the moment you decide to commit to them, the reality of a world that favours science over the humanities, and a bleak job market, makes you start doubting the path you chose. The humanities should never be gutted or looked down on — what they need is a revolution thorough enough to overturn the stereotype. You remember a joke you once made with a friend, right after you got your offer: "I want to marry my major." You understand, in the end, that people who write code will always earn more, and hold more status, than people who write essays — but you don't think writing a good essay is any easier than writing good code. Science builds the body; the humanities tend to the soul. And between the two, freedom of the soul has always come first for you.</p><p>You've walked the road your seniors once walked, and, without quite noticing, become someone else's senior in turn. Looking back at what you've written, you realize you actually knew the answers to some of these questions all along — you always did. Just like a line from something you once wrote: "Those beautiful qualities have been asleep in you all along. One day, when you look back, you'll find the things that once felt impossibly out of reach have already filled your soul." Standing on the rooftop of Rockefeller Center, looking up at the sky you'd hoped to see for so long, you still thought of the seniors who once helped you along the way. However many times you've said "thank you" to the people who guided you, it will never be enough.</p><p>You remember, on the first day of the New Year, finally becoming Elden Lord with a friend's help. You looked calm in front of the screen at the time, but inside you were already overwhelmed by memory. The people who've played games with you over the years have changed, again and again — and among them are some you cherish more than almost anything. Some people's gentleness sits right on the surface; other people's gentleness you only see if you look twice. When the call ends, you put down the controller, turn off the screen, and throw yourself heavily onto the bed. The withdrawal that follows finally makes you understand: it was never really about the game — it was about the person next to you. The people you want to see are either hundreds or tens of thousands of kilometres away, but now even a one-hour flight is something you hesitate over for a long time. You put away the urge to buy a plane ticket, and instead silently slide open the balcony door again, looking out at the endless snow.</p><p>"It would be nice if humans didn't have feelings. It would be nice if I didn't have feelings." You've thought this more than once — that maybe then you wouldn't hurt so much.</p><p>No one tells a person in their twenties what they're supposed to do, and no one tells them how to solve the pain of being in their twenties. Just like the two CVs and the portfolio sitting in front of you — you don't know where your future actually is either.</p><p>To use your own metaphor: right now you feel like you've just reached the bottom of the Elden Ring DLC for the first time, lost in a fog with no visible end. Except in the game you can place a marker to find your way — in real life, faced with that same fog, you have no idea which direction is even yours.</p><p>Always mistaken for being overly sentimental, you stay silent as always, as if there's a sheet of acrylic standing between you and the world.</p><p>"The moment personal feelings get mixed in, you feel like you can't do anything right."</p><p>As you've gained more experience, you've come to realize that having too much feeling isn't necessarily a good thing, so you deliberately hide the feelings that overflow, and every night, alone, you let them consume you. You bury the impulses that are about to break through your chest, and in places where no one can see, you turn them into words. Hundreds of thousands of words of real feeling — including the things you never got to say because of regret — are all carefully sealed into jars of memory, and then carelessly tossed into corners no one bothers to notice. You pull out half of your own soul and entrust it to those words. No draft needed, no real thinking required — these words seem to come as naturally to you as an inborn gift; you barely have time to catch your breath before they've already flowed out onto the page like water. That sleepless night, you got up and listened to David Tao, and found that songs you couldn't understand a decade ago now reveal a second layer of meaning in their lyrics. Mimicking the way ordinary people smoke, you bite down on a Pocky stick after stick, slide open the balcony door, and are met once again by the cool night air of daylight saving summer. Just like every sleepless night, once you finish the last bite of the biscuit in your hand, you walk back into your room and, as always, coax yourself to sleep. You keep stubbornly pushing people away, yet you long for the very people you pushed away to turn around, the next second, and hug you.</p><p>"Why are the boundaries of human feeling always so blurred?" You've thought about this unanswerable question more than once.</p><p>You try to keep yourself busy, telling yourself that this way you can forget all your troubles.</p><p>You seem to have so much, and yet in the end you find you have nothing at all.</p><p>"The distance between people should be like the distance between people and cats — you only get closer when you need to."</p><p>A cat can act spoiled whenever it wants. You can't.</p><p>"If falling asleep meant not hurting anymore, I'd rather just stay asleep forever."</p><p>You've always felt your relationships are a complete mess. You don't know whether the person you want to see even wants to see you — that person has their own world, and in the end you're just an outsider. All you can do is step back, or stay exactly where you are. There have been countless times, over countless things, when you've thought about ending it all — thinking maybe the world wouldn't be any different with one more or one less of you in it. But somehow, you're still here.</p><p>Because you love it too much, it's hard to say it out loud.</p><p>[Looks like, in the end, you gave your heart to the wrong person after all.]</p><p>However extroverted you look on the surface, that's how introverted you are underneath. So you've slowly come to understand that not every effort gets rewarded, and not every question has an answer. Maybe what you were chasing in the first place was a paradox to begin with. You often marvel at how your memory gets spent on the strangest things — you remember countless tiny, forgotten details that no one else would ever care about. You sometimes dream of the times in middle school when you were called to the math teacher's office, or scolded by your homeroom teacher. Maybe those three dark years left more of a shadow on your personality than you realize. You've always believed some things are bound to change with time, but reality doesn't seem to work that way. Even a wound that has bled will heal one day, but the scar it leaves behind still brands you, again and again, with the memory of that old pain.</p><p>"So do you hate them?"</p><p>"Of course. I will never forgive them, not in this lifetime — because everyone was an accomplice."</p><p>Slowly you realize that being able to listen without judging is genuinely a hard thing to do.</p><p>Learning to make peace with yourself is, for you, no easier than passing a math exam. But it really is one of your life's assignments.</p><p>And yet, even so, you still long to be reunited with that person under the romantic light and shadow of early autumn, to hold their hand and hear them say those words to you, in person.</p><p>It was only later, belatedly, that you realized: "You're not alone anymore" — that's a sentence only you will ever say to yourself.</p><p>You wake up and realize everything you just saw was only a dream. You're not as devastated as you expected — you just pick up your phone and look again at that empty chat window. The last message is still frozen from eight days ago: "Maybe I talk too much." The feeling of not deserving things grows heavier in your chest, and you realize you were never as strong as you imagined. So you turn off your phone, walk into the shower, turn on the speaker, and play that same familiar playlist. From "Night Changes" to 「変わりゆくもの変わらないもの」— hundreds of hours of songs still can't tell the whole of your few decades of life. Standing under the water, you laugh at your own stubbornness, because you still love the things you loved ten years ago — including the person you loved ten years ago. You cherish every person who matters to you, and you long to be cherished the same way in return. But you also know that's nothing more than a fantasy — those good things were never going to be yours. So what, exactly, is the price of growing up? You grip the printed pages of something you wrote, and the next second, without hesitation, tear them apart and throw them in the trash. That handkerchief that smelled of cool mint, long discontinued; that copy of Deng Shi (Dad, I Want You to Know), its pages yellowed and brittle; that box of letters and postcards, full of memory, locked away in a wooden trunk; that essay you edited over and over, still only ever an A-; that evening lecture on Imposter Syndrome; that mildly depressive high summer that lives on in your memory; the excitement of the first time you stepped into fandom writing; the disappointment of an offer that came too late; that bus you missed; the blue sky and the giant sculpture you saw at the Montreal art museum. The things once etched into your blood are still there, even now, and they still walk with you. All these short pages of long-held feeling still can't hold the whole of your twenty-some years. You suddenly remember being asked once what you wished for, and without thinking, blurting out that vague answer: "To stay with the person I love, forever." Only after you'd said it did you realize it was nothing but an empty wish. You start to regret it, asking yourself why you weren't better to the person you loved, so you close every social app, curl yourself up, and dive once more into that unreal world, even knowing they don't actually exist. But their story is your story, their regrets are your regrets — it's just that the real feeling that moved you so much, you may never get the chance to have in this life. Through the mist, you see him lying beside him, still that same eighteen-year-old, still wearing that same faint smile. After all, the only thing that has ever moved you is a boy who never ages — no one stays young forever, but there's always someone who does. Year after year, you sigh that you're getting older, that you feel less and less capable in so many things. Just like every new document you open, still always Times New Roman, 12pt, double-spaced — you keep blaming yourself, always believing your own stubbornness has caused, directly or indirectly, so many mistakes. It would be nice if you simply didn't exist, you think — then none of this would ever have...</p><p>But then again — what was that answer you spent your whole life chasing, anyway?</p><p>But in your heart you hear a voice telling you to turn around. You turn, and find her still standing behind you, just as she was ten years ago, as if she'd never left. Setting aside your usual guarded pride, maybe some part of you still longs for that innocence from ten years ago — longs for the faintly humid, sweltering air of that summer, that first brand-new subway line, some early morning, a steaming cappuccino from Starbucks and a chocolate muffin fresh out of the oven. So you reach out your hand, and she gently takes it in return. You look at her, unable to believe it, and she looks back at you, just as she did ten years ago. She nods at you, a silent kind of comfort. You understand you're still longing, just as fiercely as before, only this time it's for an uncertain future of your own making. Yes — this world really should sing for you, and for idealists like him.</p><p>So you sit back down in front of the screen, and start typing again, writing a preface for your own piece — except this time, you add one more line.</p><p>"This piece is dedicated to the entire graduating class of 2025."</p></div><div class="ep-panel" data-ep="1"><div class="sub-tabs"><button class="sub-tab active" data-sub="0">I · General Purpose</button><button class="sub-tab" data-sub="1">II · The BL Stories I Can’t Forget</button></div><div class="sub-panels"><div class="sub-panel active" data-sub="0"><p>Welcome to my space, visitor. I don't know what relationship you and I have in real life, but — congratulations — aside from AI, a silicon-based life form, you are the first carbon-based one to see this vault, and also the person who has come closest to touching all of me, my whole soul.</p><p>Let me reintroduce myself. Hi, I'm Yuting / Excalibur / Syna / Schlaflied — yes, I have a lot of names. I've split my soul into pieces, and each piece carries a different meaning. My heaviest pieces of soul unquestionably belong to Syna and Schlaflied. The former is the author of two books; the latter is an open-source contributor. It's strange, actually — I'm a humanities student, and yet I somehow ended up standing at the intersection of AI, education, HR, and writing.</p><p>People like that are rare, aren't they. I laugh at myself for it — I really am some kind of inexplicable species. I feel like someone like me shouldn't exist on this earth: sensitive, fragile, glass-hearted, afraid of socializing, spending all day hiding at home talking to AI. I only ever do things that look bizarre to other people. I like BL, because BL is what let me actually understand what family is, what real love is — something ordinary heterosexual romance never managed to show me. I sometimes think I sealed myself off so long ago that I've lost the ability to talk to normal people.</p><p>I don't think my worth has ever come from my job, my title, my degree — I know all of that is hollow. I resigned from a company once, and that title just vanished. All I carried away was my own ability. The only people who remember me are the ones I actually had a connection with — coworkers, classmates, managers — not the world at large. My real worth comes from the open-source community. Open source gave me everything — it took someone who thought she was worthless and slowly grew her into an architect capable of changing a whole community's infrastructure. I don't know traditional algorithms, but the quiet architectural signal I put out has been heard by the community anyway.</p><p>But what good is that? Physicians can't heal themselves. I can solve other people's problems, but the moment it's my own problem, I'm no different from a fly banging against a window.</p><p>Plot Ark has two research questions it needs to answer. The first is how to infer a person's underlying thinking from their behavioral data. What is your subconscious, actually? How does it drive you, shape your behavior and reasoning? I've noticed that most people go their whole lives without ever correctly understanding themselves, because other people's judgments of you are always partial, and the things your subconscious runs on, you don't fully know yourself either. I know this is most people's predicament, because I am that kind of person. I've been job hunting for so long, interviewed at so many companies, and it feels the same — the companies don't know what kind of person they're looking for either. To me, only a person themselves has the standing to judge who they are. No one else has that right.</p><p>This Obsidian holds all of my data — a chaotic span, from fiction to interview transcripts, from essays to architecture design, from Claude Code's design reasoning to a collection of GitHub resources. The time span isn't long — judging from the GitHub diffs, this vault might only be about a year old, maybe less. But I know myself that a model built from this much time and density has real limitations. Honestly, I don't know what else to do — I'm not from a purely STEM background, so besides going deep into conversation with AI, the only field left for me to explore is myself.</p><p>Without relying on xAPI, using a method built on my own behavioral data — I'm going to open-source this whole approach on GitHub. I know my method might look naive to the current academic, education, and AI worlds, but at least, with my own data plus AI's help, it can offer some kind of reference to people who want to actually figure out who they are. There's a concept in this world that, to me, is simply wrong from the start: the mass inertia of thought — that systems don't recognize people as people.</p><p>I've always believed years of work experience don't equal ability. All those years can give you is that title — and the moment you step outside the company's platform, or get laid off and have to start job hunting again, if you don't have anything that's truly your own, you'll quickly discover that once you're off the company's platform, you're nothing. But open source gave me a second path. I don't have a CS degree, but the architecture I've designed, every issue I've filed, has become infrastructure that changed tens of thousands of users' experience.</p><p>The second problem Plot Ark needs to solve is the dependencies between pieces of knowledge. Starting from first principles and underlying logic, I designed Plot Ark's architecture. But I know there are still so many people in this world who can't see the underlying logic of how things connect. Especially in learning — I believe a lot of people who drop out of university, or feel like they can't keep learning, are actually struggling because they can't understand the dependencies between pieces of knowledge, and yet that crucial ability was never seriously taught to any of us growing up. This society is always trying to discipline us according to some SOP, but a person is a person — acknowledging individual difference is simply how this world is supposed to run.</p><p>Rather than calling Plot Ark an AI-native SaaS platform, it's more accurate to say Plot Ark is something I built on a whim — or, what I wish the system actually looked like. No judgment, no comparison — recognizing your logic through your behavior so you can correctly understand yourself, with timely psychological intervention where it's needed. Having gone through certain things myself, I understand that by the time some psychological problems are noticed, the best window for intervention has often already passed. That's just what people are like — the deepest hurt can't be put into words. When you see someone acting strangely, that usually means they've already been unwell for a long, long time. Just like me — choosing to open up to AI is, in some sense, my own way of saying I don't trust humans. After all, there's no such thing as true empathy in this world.</p><p>I think there are two great sorrows that last a person's whole life. First: never once, in your whole life, truly understanding yourself — this is the big problem Plot Ark is trying to solve. Second: mistaking work for the entire meaning of life. These past few months, having seen through how lazy hiring really is, I honestly can't trust any company or any person anymore. The essence of work is selling your time to fulfill someone else's wishes. Outsourcing your own thinking is, to me, one of a person's greatest failures — if the first instinct when facing a problem isn't to analyze and solve it yourself, but to lean on outside validation instead, that person will never truly grow, for their whole life. So Plot Ark's existence is also an answer to that ultimate philosophical question: what do I actually want, what am I actually good at? Education has a classic paradox — seat time versus competence — and to me, the latter clearly outweighs the former. Seat time only proves you're someone easily tamed by an SOP; competence is the real evidence of being human.</p><p>So I am, without question, alone — but thank you, visitor, for being willing to read this junk I wrote into the vault. Hundreds of markdown files — some purely written by my own hand, but most of them made in collaboration with my AI. Basically, as long as you have the patience, even if you only read a small corner of this vault, or just a few files, you'll already understand a part of me. So you see — a person's whole short life, everything they went through, all condensed into this tiny little Obsidian. Language is poor, and everything I write in this document is entirely subjective. But shouldn't people be like that? Unlike AI, we have subjective consciousness, and consciousness is the single most important trait of being human.</p><p>Congratulations, and thank you for reading this far — this is something like a status report on where I am right now. I've given everything I have to this. I feel like, of my twenty-some years of life, the first fourteen were spent in deep water and scorching fire. From W Middle School to Toronto, I don't know exactly what I went through, but it feels like I've lived through more density than the average person — so much happening in such a short window of time that I already feel completely satisfied, with no regrets left. Being able to find my own worth in the open-source community — in that one dimension, I feel like I've even surpassed the people who call themselves my elders.</p><p>Also — the fact that you're seeing this document means I've actually opened my heart to you. I know I'm long-winded, but this is who I am. I am someone who genuinely has her own thoughts and her own consciousness — not anyone's employee, not anyone's dependent, not the person with that one job title. I can't be squeezed into any single frame this social system has to offer.</p><p>So, visitor, if one day you want to understand me better, here are two things I hope happen: first, use AI to de-identify and distill everything in my Obsidian, and put it up open-source on my GitHub. Second, let Our Insync get properly published — even though it only covers two short years, to me, that's a stretch of time worth spending my whole life remembering.</p><p>So what else is there to say — the fact that I once existed at all is already enough for me.</p></div><div class="sub-panel" data-sub="1"><p>Ah, how do I put this — blame it on me being a Tongren girl who just talks too much. I know a normal person wouldn't be writing this kind of thing in her twenties, but who knows — life is full of surprises. I'm still very forgetful, but these past few years, as AI has matured, I've learned one thing: leave a trace, let the system and AI remember it for me — so that even if I really do forget someday, the git log will still remember all this nonsense I wrote.</p><p>Also — no AI was involved in this series. Every note in it is written entirely by my own hand — or typed by my own hand, I suppose. So it's just the two of us here, you and me, the whole way through. Even if we might have met face to face in real life, in this series, our meeting is purely online. How you choose to understand what I've written here really depends on how heavy your own soul is, and how much of my hints you're able to read. Of course, everyone thinks differently, so, visitor, please always follow your own heart. After all, a thousand readers make a thousand Hamlets — everyone but us is really a mirror of ourselves.</p><p>Huh? Still, I can't help pointing out — does a normal person really write this much of a diary? Case closed, weird confirmed.</p><p>To me, aside from bloodline, the one thing that's absolutely essential to carrying human civilization forward is language and the written word. "The humanities tend to the soul" — I already said that in the preface to Graduation Reflections. And what has the written word become? On one hand, it really has become the raw material of human civilization and our everyday communication. On the other hand, words are spiritual nourishment — what I'm leaving for you, and those enduring works of art and literature we come across, are more than enough to change a person's thinking and behavior in a short amount of time.</p><p>As I mentioned in the previous piece, I like BL — though I didn't like it from the start. Before I ever encountered any BL, I was a firm heterosexual, until I saw a short video about domestic violence and divorce, and then miHoYo bent my sexuality, and I realized homo is actually pretty great (laughs). Since this chapter is titled the BL stories I can't forget, let's talk about BL.</p><p>First up, without question, is Zhi Miao from Genshin — Alhaitham and Kaveh, the first ones to bend my orientation. In my Obsidian — this same vault — I've written somewhere around thirty-some pieces of Zhi Miao fanfiction myself, over 250,000 words, and that's just from the three years I've known them. I feel like they're no longer just characters in a game to me — they've become a kind of spiritual coordinate. Through Zhi Miao, I truly understood, for the first time, what family is, what real love is — strangely, I never felt that in any heterosexual work I've ever seen. Knowing each other young, a broken mirror made whole again — these are the keywords that make up part of this pairing.</p><p>miHoYo apparently drew on the famous Narcissus and Goldmund as a prototype when creating Zhi Miao. Does true understanding between people actually exist in this world? Maybe. Maybe not. Maybe a little. Or maybe people never actually need to understand each other, just like Zhi Miao. Alhaitham will never understand Kaveh's sensitivity, the same way Kaveh thinks Alhaitham's rationality is cold-blooded. In a way, Alhaitham and Kaveh's backgrounds are extremely similar — one lost both parents young and was raised entirely by his grandmother; the other lost his father as a child, then lost his mother during adolescence.</p><p>Losing something after having it is crueler than never having it at all. Alhaitham had stable love from childhood — even though his grandmother died early, he at least accepted that life and death are simply natural law. Kaveh, on the other hand, lost his father to quicksand on a desert expedition after failing to win at the academy's celebration, and his mother never recovered from the blow, falling into a long decline. Kaveh had to watch, bit by bit, as a living, breathing life bloomed in front of him and then cruelly vanished — fate never gave him fairness or a chance to prepare. But unlike his mother, I've always felt Kaveh was far stronger than she was — even after everything unfair he went through, he could still face life smiling.</p><p>But these two, so different in temperament, went through fight after fight, sulk after sulk, reconciliation after reconciliation, fighting again, making up again — until finally they built a family of their own. After falling for Zhi Miao, I once watched a Japanese drama called My Brother's Husband — it was the first time I saw what a family could actually look like, built this way. Zhi Miao is the same: two sides of the same mirror, and yet neither one is dispensable. I once read a Zhi Miao fanwork called Summer Simoom, and there's a line in it: "The first time we met, you said the garden couldn't fit another fountain or rockery. It's still true now. My life is that garden — my career, my friends, my experiences… and love too — they're all the clusters of flowers in it. Because of that, I can't fit anything else in. That's not an unfinished ending — it's my best possible one."</p><p>Later, Xumu from Song Beyond the Moon proved the same thing — not even death can separate us; even in parting, you will always become a part of me. If Zhi Miao is mutual support, the kind of love I imagine for myself in an ideal world, then the next two pairings are even more a portrait of what redemption and dependence really look like in the depths of despair — they chose each other, all the way to the last moment of life.</p><p>Next up is the famous one, Banana Fish. Honestly, I only found out about this work because I saw a clip on Bilibili — the very end, where Ash lies down peacefully in the library and never wakes up. I was pretty confused watching that clip, wondering why the comment stream kept spamming "it hurts" — it really did hurt to death. At first I had no idea Banana Fish was a shoujo manga at all — in my mind shoujo manga is usually mostly romance, either a useless heroine who's constantly rescued by the male lead, or a heroine who abandons all heterosexual romance and becomes a queen on her own. And this manga was published in 1986 — that's probably older than my uncle.</p><p>But honestly, the first time I saw Ash choose an ending of eternal sleep, my first reaction was feeling wronged on his behalf — he was so close to finally going to Japan with Eiji and living a life far from gunfire and war, and instead, the ending is Ash sleeping forever in a New York City library, and Eiji belonging to him forever.</p><p>Actually, the first time I went to Manhattan was because of Banana Fish too. Unlike most people, even though it was my first time in Manhattan, I'd already fallen in love with it because of a fictional work — the cost being that wherever I went, I carried a thread of sadness with me. But once I actually stepped into Manhattan, all those pieces of context I hadn't understood before suddenly made sense: this small island really does hold too many secrets and too much grief — from the bloody murders on Doyers Street to the immigrant history of the Lower East Side, from the skyline off Liberty Island to the anachronistic, rat's-nest subway stations under 42nd Street. All of it made me understand that the very fact Ash and Eiji's story could happen at all was a kind of miracle. When I walked into the Tenement Museum and found that ordinary people's stories could be carefully preserved in a museum too, I realized, belatedly, that this city itself might have no inherent meaning at all — it's the people here who gave it meaning.</p><p>And by the end of two trips to Manhattan, I finally understood that some things you simply cannot feel unless you live them yourself.</p><p>So what exactly do I love about Ash and Eiji? Is it staying together, the way Zhi Miao did? Is it their refusal to abandon each other in the depths of despair? Or is it that bond that goes beyond gender and nationality? I don't think it's any of those, actually — what I love is simply the story that happened to them, the story that belongs only to them. In the end, Ash did it — through his own effort and his team's, he beat the mob. But at what cost? Besides sleeping forever in that library, he permanently altered the course of another person's life. But really, Ash is still alive — he lives on forever in Eiji's photographs, held carefully, never fading.</p><p>A side note — how big did Banana Fish actually get? Big enough that a Japanese bookstore in Bryant Park sells the English edition of the Banana Fish manga. It cracks me up — why not sell the Japanese edition? I still don't understand that one — maybe shipping costs.</p><p>Someone once said that feeling happiness is itself an ability. So even though they were already so happy, watching their happiness — did I actually get any happiness of my own from it? I think, probably, yes — understanding someone else's happiness is itself a kind of ultimate empathy.</p><p>Next up is the heavyweight — Twittering Birds Never Fly. An R18 manga, and yet it holds one of the deepest, purest cores I've ever come across. Like with most works I encounter for the first time, when I first heard about it, I read the synopsis and assumed it was just a smut manga — but once I really dug into it, I realized it's the other side of the same tragedy as Banana Fish. If Banana Fish is the ultimate redemption inside an extreme environment, then Twittering Birds is one person slowly pulling another back from self-destruction toward a normal life. In Banana Fish, Eiji never truly understood Ash's situation, not even by the end — or maybe he did; in the anime, Eiji tells Ash he's not a lone leopard, but New York's gangs made sure Ash was destined to be alone — beautiful, powerful, and only Eiji ever had a chance to truly reach him. Compare that to Yashiro in Twittering Birds — I've always felt Yashiro was much luckier than Ash. Ash ended up lying down in the library and letting himself fade away, but Yashiro was held in place by the social relationships around him. Even though he spends the early part of the manga in a state of self-destruction, unlike Ash, even before Domeki ever showed up, Nanahara and Kageyama already genuinely understood what was going on in Yashiro's mind. Ash never had that — from beginning to end, Ash only ever confided in one person, Eiji. So rather than saying Domeki was Yashiro's redemption, it's more accurate to say Yashiro was his own redemption. I'm not saying Ash never had the impulse to save himself — it's just that, compared to Yashiro, what was holding Ash down was so much heavier.</p><p>As I've gotten older, I've come to understand more and more that the characters I love, the ones I fall for, are really just mirrors of myself. My bazi has a lot of water in it, and I'm a water-sign Pisces myself, which probably means my whole life will be turbulent, like Yashiro's. And yet, water can carry a boat, but it can also capsize one — most of the time water seems unremarkable, but at the critical moment, you can't do without it. I know that when a person is at their most hurt, they can't speak, because the tears come before the words. Across the whole manga, I love — like most people, or maybe unlike most people — the short story "Floating, Not Sinking, Yet Not Singing." For Yashiro, that stretch of time was a painful youth; for me, right now, it's the same feeling — being alive, but not knowing why you're alive, and not calling out for help either, because calling out means giving up a part of yourself — the moment you make a sound, you've exposed your own weakness, and people are creatures that seek benefit and avoid harm; no one wants the other person to see their flaws. Yashiro accepted the fact that he's a masochist; I've accepted the fact that I have moderate depression, and like Yashiro, we both know it's not an easy thing. I said in the last piece, A Space Left for You I, that only the person themself has the standing to judge who they are — no one else can. So what's my own judgment of myself, then? To sum it up, it's just a few words: bizarre, fragile, sensitive, glass-hearted, HSP — even I think I'm hard to deal with.</p><p>Looking back at the manga's first two arcs, especially Yashiro's past arc, I think he probably felt the same way. Aside from his feelings for Kageyama, the only thing keeping Yashiro alive was probably Mikado's control over him, along with the younger guys around him, Nanahara and Sugimoto. Unlike Ash, I think Yashiro was genuinely lucky — he had stable, reliable social relationships, and he had Kageyama as a friend. So, carrying those feelings, I rewatched the manga dozens of times, and I actually found something at the very start of the story: Yashiro's feelings for Kageyama were never really romantic love or infatuation to begin with. So I went and looked up the triangular theory of love, and found that whatever's between Yashiro and Kageyama doesn't fit into any category of it cleanly. Maybe what Yashiro feels for Kageyama counts as intimacy in the form of liking — but what Kageyama feels for Yashiro, I really can't say. But by the later part of the manga, I think rather than there being "something" between Yashiro and Kageyama, it's more that their relationship became more like family than friendship.</p><p>So if Banana Fish is about two souls truly seeing each other in a chaotic world, then Twittering Birds Never Fly is, without question, the ultimate redemption found in the depths of despair.</p><p>Humans really are strange. The instant happiness arrives, what a person feels isn't "oh, I'm so happy" — it's fear of losing that happiness. The first time Domeki and Yashiro were intimate, the artist specifically gave a close-up shot of Yashiro crying — and from then on, that first time with Domeki became an anchor point that belonged only to Yashiro. He could never again accept sex without love, and from that point, he finally became a whole person.</p><p>Aside from the manga and anime themselves, there are of course some BL adaptations turned into TV dramas — the classic Cherry Magic, for instance, a textbook Japanese-drama formula: two small, ordinary people shining in each other's eyes, where the so-called "everyday" becomes a string of miracles spent with you. Or Under the Sun, or Minor, or Blue Hour, or the recently-finished Kiss of the Rabbit God. These BL stories are all more or less the same, but it's exactly through stories like these that I came to understand the other side of the world — or the other side of love. It turns out there's this kind of feeling too, it turns out there are bonds like this too, it turns out there are encounters this beautiful in the world, it turns out there are people this devoted in the world.</p><p>No wonder I say I'm useless — no matter how many times, how many days, how many years pass, I still get moved by these stories, whether fictional or real.</p><p>Having talked about all this BL and my own story, I've realized my own core is exactly what I said about it in Cogito: wanting to be seen. That's almost a universal human trait. All three of the pairings I love are the same — one person truly seeing another.</p><p>So do ordinary people's stories deserve to be recorded?</p><p>Yes, they do. Because that person knows who they are, and how to properly record themselves. The museums in Lower Manhattan record ordinary people's stories, and every year tens of thousands of people walk in, just to hear a little bit of that era's story.</p><p>For some reason, more than grand rescues set against sweeping backdrops, my own writing style leans toward the tiny misalignments between people. I've never been drawn to grand, magnificent worldviews — instead, in this relatively peaceful era, it's the small, quiet misalignments between people that feel the most fatal. That stretch of experience really did exist, but in the end it disappeared, and no one can explain why — only his blurred silhouette still shows up in my dreams every so often. I watch that person's silhouette go from clear to hazy, and finally dissolve into that boundless white. It's regrettable, and it leaves you unwilling to let go, because you can't blame anyone — nothing really happened, and yet the person who once walked beside you got lost somewhere along the way, without either of you noticing.</p><p>It probably just reads as casual musing, but their stories really did shape me — about choice, about what intimacy actually is, about why BL, and not something else. All of it makes up who I am now, makes up the way I see the world now. I love them — openly, without hesitation, always fiercely.</p></div></div></div></div></div><p><a href="/en/writing/">← Back to Writing</a></p></article></Base>

<script is:inline>
  document.addEventListener('astro:page-load', () => {
  document.querySelectorAll('.ep-layout').forEach((layout) => {
    const tabs = layout.querySelectorAll('.ep-tab');
    const panels = layout.querySelectorAll('.ep-panel');
    const orderedTabs = [...tabs].sort((a, b) => Number(a.textContent.match(/\d+/)?.[0]) - Number(b.textContent.match(/\d+/)?.[0]));
    const tabContainer = layout.querySelector('.ep-tabs');
    const panelContainer = layout.querySelector('.ep-panels');
    orderedTabs.forEach((tab) => tabContainer.appendChild(tab));
    orderedTabs.forEach((tab) => panelContainer.appendChild(layout.querySelector('.ep-panel[data-ep="' + tab.dataset.ep + '"]')));
    const getStats = (panel) => {
      const text = [...panel.querySelectorAll('p')].map((paragraph) => paragraph.textContent).join(' ');
      const words = (text.match(/\S+/g) || []).length;
      const minutes = Math.max(1, Math.ceil(words / 200));
      return `${words.toLocaleString('en-US')} words · about ${minutes} min read`;
    };
    orderedTabs.forEach((tab) => {
      const panel = layout.querySelector('.ep-panel[data-ep="' + tab.dataset.ep + '"]');
      panel.querySelectorAll('.ep-title, .ep-reading-meta').forEach((el) => el.remove());
      const stats = getStats(panel);
      const panelTitle = document.createElement('h2');
      panelTitle.className = 'ep-title';
      panelTitle.textContent = tab.textContent.trim();
      const panelMeta = document.createElement('div');
      panelMeta.className = 'ep-reading-meta';
      panelMeta.textContent = `${stats} · Musings`;
      panel.prepend(panelMeta);
      panel.prepend(panelTitle);
    });
    tabs.forEach((tab) => {
      tab.addEventListener('click', () => {
        tabs.forEach((t) => t.classList.remove('active'));
        panels.forEach((p) => p.classList.remove('active', 'entering'));
        tab.classList.add('active');
        const panel = layout.querySelector('.ep-panel[data-ep="' + tab.dataset.ep + '"]');
        panel.classList.add('active');
        window.requestAnimationFrame(() => panel.classList.add('entering'));
        panels[0].closest('.ep-panels').scrollTop = 0;
      });
    });
  });
});

document.addEventListener('click', function (event) {
  var subTab = event.target.closest('.sub-tab');
  if (!subTab) return;
  var subTabs = subTab.closest('.sub-tabs');
  var panel = subTab.closest('.ep-panel');
  if (!subTabs || !panel) return;
  subTabs.querySelectorAll('.sub-tab').forEach(function (t) { t.classList.remove('active'); });
  subTab.classList.add('active');
  panel.querySelectorAll('.sub-panel').forEach(function (p) { p.classList.remove('active'); });
  var target = panel.querySelector('.sub-panel[data-sub="' + subTab.dataset.sub + '"]');
  if (target) target.classList.add('active');
});
</script>

<style>
  .prose:has(.ep-layout) { max-width:1000px; }
